  "cli_result_text": "Submitted. Summary of the trace these files support:\n\n**Netty side (exception origin):**\n- `DefaultChannelPipeline.java` \u2014 owns `fireExceptionCaught`, and its `TailContext` swallows unhandled exceptions with only a logger warning (\"An exceptionCaught() event was fired, and it reached at the tail of the pipeline\") \u2014 the first point context can be silently dropped.\n- `AbstractChannelHandlerContext.java` \u2014 `invokeExceptionCaught`/`notifyHandlerException` propagate the `Throwable` handler-to-handler; no structured context (request ID, remote address) travels with it, only the raw exception.\n- `ChannelPipeline.java` \u2014 interface contract for `fireExceptionCaught`.\n- `ChannelHandlerContext.java`, `handler/ssl/SslHandler.java` \u2014 connection-reset/SSL-layer exceptions typically enter the pipeline here and get wrapped/rethrown via `exceptionCaught`.\n\n**Bridge (reactor-netty \u2192 Spring):**\n- `ReactorHttpHandlerAdapter.java` \u2014 adapts reactor-netty's `HttpServerRequest/Response` to Spring's `HttpHandler`; only catches `URISyntaxException` explicitly, everything else just gets `doOnError` logging (message only, no cause chain preserved downstream) before the error propagates as a raw `Throwable`.\n\n**Spring WebFlux side (where context is lost):**\n- `WebHttpHandlerBuilder.java` \u2014 assembles the `WebExceptionHandler` chain around the `WebHandler`, wraps it in `HttpWebHandlerAdapter`.\n- `ExceptionHandlingWebHandler.java` \u2014 runs each `WebExceptionHandler` via `onErrorResume`; if none matches, the exception falls through.\n- `ResponseStatusExceptionHandler.java` \u2014 only understands `ResponseStatusException`; anything else (including Netty-originated connection errors) passes through unhandled.\n- `HttpWebHandlerAdapter.handleUnresolvedError()` \u2014 **this is the actual translation-to-500 point**: sets `INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR`, logs the exception server-side, but the client only ever sees a bare 500 \u2014 the original Netty-level cause (e.g., connection reset) is never surfaced in the response, only in server logs, and is indistinguishable from any other unresolved error.\n- `ReactorServerHttpResponse.java` \u2014 where `response.send()` hands bytes back to reactor-netty/Netty, closing the loop.",
